## Approach
Convert to a char array and swap from both ends toward the middle, using two index pointers. No new string or buffer is allocated, so the only extra space is the two int indices.

## Code
```java
public static String reverse(String s) {
    char[] c = s.toCharArray();
    int i = 0, j = c.length - 1;
    while (i < j) {
        char t = c[i];
        c[i] = c[j];
        c[j] = t;
        i++;
        j--;
    }
    return new String(c);
}
```

## Complexity
Time O(n), one pass over half the array. Space O(1) extra beyond the char array, which is the input's own mutable copy.

One caveat: `String` is immutable in Java, so a truly in-place reverse needs a `char[]` or `StringBuilder` you own. If the signature takes `char[]`, drop the `toCharArray` and `new String` and swap directly on the passed array.

