items.answer:
The bug is a data race: all 100 goroutines read and write `total` with no synchronization, so increments get lost. There's also a closure bug, since `i` is captured by reference and may be read after the loop advances.

```go
func main() {
	var wg sync.WaitGroup
	var mu sync.Mutex
	total := 0
	for i := 1; i <= 100; i++ {
		wg.Add(1)
		go func(i int) {
			defer wg.Done()
			mu.Lock()
			total += i
			mu.Unlock()
		}(i)
	}
	wg.Wait()
	fmt.Println(total)
}
```

Passing `i` as a parameter fixes the capture, and the mutex serializes the writes. For a single counter, an atomic is the better fix: `atomic.AddInt64(&total, int64(i))` is lock-free and cheaper than a mutex, which only pays off when you're protecting a larger critical section.

## Approach
The interviewer's two statements conflict: they said the array is already sorted ascending, then asked for the kth largest in an unsorted array. I'll take the unsorted version as the real problem, since that's the one where "better than sorting" matters. A min-heap of size k gives O(n log k), and quickselect gives O(n) average.

## Code
```python
import heapq
import random

def find_kth_largest(nums, k):
    # Min-heap of size k: the root is the kth largest seen so far.
    heap = nums[:k]
    heapq.heapify(heap)
    for x in nums[k:]:
        if x > heap[0]:
            heapq.heapreplace(heap, x)
    return heap[0]

def find_kth_largest_quickselect(nums, k):
    target = len(nums) - k  # index in ascending order

    def select(lo, hi):
        pivot = nums[random.randint(lo, hi)]
        i, j = lo, hi
        while i <= j:
            while nums[i] < pivot:
                i += 1
            while nums[j] > pivot:
                j -= 1
            if i <= j:
                nums[i], nums[j] = nums[j], nums[i]
                i += 1
                j -= 1
        if target <= j:
            return select(lo, j)
        if target >= i:
            return select(i, hi)
        return nums[target]

    return select(0, len(nums) - 1)
```

## Complexity
Min-heap: O(n log k) time, O(k) space. Quickselect: O(n) average, O(n²) worst case, O(1) extra space.
